 :correction-1-the-batching-win-was-overstated
 "I reported ~11.5x for the join work (2026-08-02-ic09-after-hash-join.edn). It is about 3.5x.

  The baseline that number rested on -- 35,641 ms for IC09 execute, from 2026-08-01-ic09-diagnosis.edn -- was taken on a host at load average 150-190. The SAME pre-batching code, measured here on a quiet machine, does it in 5,270 ms. The baseline was inflated roughly sevenfold by the host, and every ratio computed against it inherited that.

  Load-corrected against planning (unchanged code in both runs, 928.9 -> 1153.3 ms, so this run's host was ~1.24x slower): execute went 5,270.6 -> 1,872.8 ms raw, 2.81x, times 1.24 = about 3.5x attributable to the change.

  The load control I built caught inflation twice and still missed this, because it corrects WITHIN a pair of runs -- it cannot tell you the pair itself was taken somewhere unrepresentative. I compared one version measured fresh against another version remembered."

 :correction-2-ic02-did-not-regress
 "I reported IC02 as a regression I introduced (7.7x -> 14.7x -> 19.2x of Neo4j across three LDBC runs) and spent two changes on it, one shipped and reverted.

  It does not appear here. IC02 issues exactly 1,793 scans returning exactly 2,702 rows on BOTH pins -- the change does not touch what it asks the store. Execute went 98.5 -> 197.8 ms raw, 2.01x, against a host its own planning says was 1.87x slower (29.8 -> 55.8 ms for identical planner code). That leaves about 1.07x: unchanged.

  What moved in the LDBC receipts was a ratio whose denominator is Neo4j's IC02 at 5 ms -- too small and too different a workload to control a kotobase query dominated by planner probes. IC02 was never the regression I thought it was, and neither explanation I gave for it was answering a real question."

 :what-the-instrumentation-actually-shows
 ["162x fewer scans (143,270 -> 885) is the load-invariant product of batching plus hash join. It does not move with the host, and it is the number worth quoting."
  "Only 9% of IC09 execute is now inside the store (174 of 1,873 ms), down from 81%. Scan-side work is finished as a source of wins; what remains is the join's own bookkeeping -- grouping, hash index construction, unification, set building."
  "The planner's probes do not go through scan* at all (0 counted, yet 1,153 ms of planning wall for IC09) -- they call arrangement.query/query directly. Planning is now 38% of IC09's total, and all of it is scanning to learn a number :query-statistics could supply."
  "Rows returned fell 230,321 -> 70,927, so the hash join is not only batching requests -- it stops re-fetching the same rows for different bindings."]

 :next
 "Two things, in order. First :query-statistics: planning is 38% of IC09 and is pure waste. Second the join's own bookkeeping, now 91% of execute.

  And a method fix: never compare a fresh measurement against a remembered one. Both versions, same machine, same session, or it is not a comparison."

 :caveats
 ["Single run per pin, one start person. Counts are exact and load-invariant; millisecond figures are within-run ratios only."
  "The counter itself adds per-call overhead, which inflates the OLD pin more than the NEW (143,270 calls against 885) -- so 3.5x is if anything generous to the change."
  "The planner's probes are invisible to this counter because they bypass scan*; their cost appears only as planning wall time."]

